      {
        "evidence_id": "E005",
        "passage_id": "P002",
        "quote": "Plaintiff's reliance on the affidavit of her expert, who stated that the design and construction of the laundry room ventilation system created a dangerous, slippery condition on the floor, is misplaced because as noted by the motion court, the expert did not demonstrate that the testing he performed sufficiently replicated the conditions in the laundry room on the day of the accident, which was five months earlier",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E006",
        "passage_id": "P003",
        "quote": "The expert also lacked the expertise to offer his opinion with respect to the ventilation system in the laundry room ( see Schechter v 3320 Holding LLC , 64 AD3d 446 , 449-450 [1st Dept 2009]). Even assuming plaintiff's expert was qualified to render an expert opinion, it is noted that his affidavit states that he touched the laundry room floor with his hand and found that it was \"wet and damp.\" However, he did not state that the floor was wet in the area where plaintiff fell, and, \"an expert's examination of a part of the general area is insufficient to preclude summary judgment\"",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      }
